1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Hi, and welcome back to Act. Think about a
problem

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,400
you see around you. Maybe it happens in
your neighborhood,

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:10,040
your university, your workplace, or
somewhere else in your community.

4
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:13,720
Before trying to fix it, there is one
important step:

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,960
make sure you understand what the problem
really is. A

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,520
social issue is simply a problem that
affects a group

7
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,660
of people or a community. It is easy to
jump

8
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:27,040
straight to a solution because that feels
active. But if

9
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,640
the problem is not clear, the solution can
miss the

10
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:33,460
point. So start with a few simple
questions: what is

11
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:37,100
happening, who is affected, where is it
happening, and is

12
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:39,380
it getting better, worse, or staying the
same?

13
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,100
Here’s a real example. A young man lived
in a

14
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:46,820
place where lions were attacking
livestock. He wanted to protect

15
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,720
the animals, but his first ideas did not
work. Instead

16
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:54,540
of giving up, he kept watching, learning,
and trying again

17
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:59,340
until he understood the problem better.
Pause here and watch

18
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:02,100
the linked video about how he made peace
with lions.

19
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:06,660
While you watch, notice what changed
between his first attempts

20
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:10,540
and the solution that finally worked. The
big lesson is

21
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:14,860
simple: wanting to help is not enough by
itself. You

22
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:18,240
also need to understand the problem, learn
from what is

23
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:21,020
not working, and be ready to change your
approach.

24
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,220
Now bring this back to your own community.
Look at

25
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:28,000
the problems you noticed before and choose
one that you

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,340
really want to understand better. It does
not have to

27
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,180
be the biggest problem around you. It just
needs to

28
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,980
be real, clear enough to study, and
important enough that

29
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,480
you care about it. Try not to choose
something only

30
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:45,420
because it sounds serious. Choose
something you can actually look

31
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:49,640
into. Ask yourself: who is affected by
this, can I

32
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,620
find useful information about it, and do I
want to

33
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:55,760
stay with this issue for the next few
lessons? Pick

34
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,700
one issue and write it down. That will be
the

35
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:00,080
problem you work with from here.

36
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:05,027
Next, make the issue more specific. A
strong issue statement

37
00:02:05,027 --> 00:02:09,787
answers five simple things. First: what is
changing? Is the

38
00:02:09,787 --> 00:02:15,047
problem increasing, decreasing, spreading,
or staying the same? Second: what

39
00:02:15,047 --> 00:02:20,007
exactly is the problem? Third: who is
affected? Fourth: where

40
00:02:20,007 --> 00:02:23,607
is it happening? Fifth: when is it
happening, if time

41
00:02:23,607 --> 00:02:27,327
matters. You do not need fancy words. You
just need

42
00:02:27,327 --> 00:02:30,567
a sentence that helps another person
understand the problem quickly.

43
00:02:31,047 --> 00:02:35,767
Compare these two: “Smoking is a problem”
and “Smoking is

44
00:02:35,767 --> 00:02:39,607
increasing among teenagers in this area.”
The second one gives

45
00:02:39,607 --> 00:02:42,827
you much more direction for research and
later for choosing

46
00:02:42,827 --> 00:02:43,607
a useful response.

47
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:48,180
Let’s look at one example: “The increase
in the number

48
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:52,260
of high school dropouts in Jeddah in
2015.” Can you

49
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:56,500
spot the five parts? “Increase” tells us
how the problem

50
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:00,180
is changing. “High school dropouts” tells
us what the issue

51
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:04,880
is. “High school students” tells us who is
affected. “Jeddah”

52
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:10,260
tells us where. “2015” tells us when. That
is much

53
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:14,560
clearer than simply saying, “Students are
leaving school.” A clear

54
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:17,960
sentence makes the next steps easier
because you know what

55
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:21,240
information to search for, who you should
learn from, and

56
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,580
what part of the problem you are actually
trying to

57
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:24,040
understand.

58
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:27,940
Now try it with your own issue. Write one
sentence

59
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:32,000
that includes: what is changing, what the
problem is, who

60
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,560
is affected, where it happens, and when it
happens if

61
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:39,040
that matters. Once you finish, read it
again as if

62
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,300
someone else wrote it. Would you
understand the problem from

63
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:46,100
that one sentence? Is anything too broad?
Is anything missing?

64
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:50,100
If yes, fix it. Do not worry about making
it

65
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:52,720
perfect on the first try. The goal is to
make

66
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,220
it clearer than before. A good issue
statement gives your

67
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:59,740
research a direction. A weak one leaves
you searching everywhere

68
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:02,320
and still not knowing what you are really
trying to

69
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:02,560
solve.

70
00:04:02,967 --> 00:04:06,287
After that, spend some time learning more
about the issue.

71
00:04:07,047 --> 00:04:09,487
Find out what it looks like in real life,
how

72
00:04:09,487 --> 00:04:12,727
it affects people, and what may happen if
nothing changes.

73
00:04:13,487 --> 00:04:18,527
You can use reliable websites, reports,
local information, or conversations

74
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:22,367
with people who understand the situation.
If the issue is

75
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:26,247
visible and you want to take photos, be
respectful. Ask

76
00:04:26,247 --> 00:04:31,027
permission before taking or sharing photos
of identifiable people. Most

77
00:04:31,027 --> 00:04:34,607
importantly, do not collect information
just to fill a page.

78
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:38,067
Use it to check your own thinking. If what
you

79
00:04:38,067 --> 00:04:40,507
find shows that your first idea about the
problem was

80
00:04:40,507 --> 00:04:45,127
wrong, that is useful. Change your
understanding instead of forcing

81
00:04:45,127 --> 00:04:46,147
the facts to fit it.

82
00:04:46,867 --> 00:04:51,107
When you finish your research, keep your
notes simple. Write

83
00:04:51,107 --> 00:04:54,887
down: what the issue is, who it affects,
how it

84
00:04:54,887 --> 00:04:59,087
affects them, and the strongest
information you found. If you

85
00:04:59,087 --> 00:05:02,467
notice that your original issue statement
is too wide or

86
00:05:02,467 --> 00:05:07,107
not quite right, improve it. That is
completely normal. Good

87
00:05:07,107 --> 00:05:11,527
research should make your thinking
clearer, not just longer. You

88
00:05:11,527 --> 00:05:14,027
are not trying to sound like an expert.
You are

89
00:05:14,027 --> 00:05:17,067
trying to understand the problem well
enough that the next

90
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:21,087
step makes sense. The more clearly you
understand the issue

91
00:05:21,087 --> 00:05:24,267
now, the less likely you are to waste time
later

92
00:05:24,267 --> 00:05:27,507
on a solution that does not match what
people actually

93
00:05:27,507 --> 00:05:27,947
need.

94
00:05:28,433 --> 00:05:33,133
By this point, you should have three
things: one issue

95
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:36,813
you want to work on, one clear sentence
that describes

96
00:05:36,813 --> 00:05:40,893
it, and some useful information that helps
you understand it

97
00:05:40,893 --> 00:05:45,413
better. That is enough for now. The next
question is

98
00:05:45,413 --> 00:05:49,413
even more important: what do the people
affected by this

99
00:05:49,413 --> 00:05:53,693
issue actually need? A problem tells you
what is going

100
00:05:53,693 --> 00:05:58,233
wrong. A need tells you what would help.
Those two

101
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:02,033
things are connected, but they are not the
same. In

102
00:06:02,033 --> 00:06:05,373
the next part, we will focus on that
difference so

103
00:06:05,373 --> 00:06:07,953
you do not jump too quickly from “there is
a

104
00:06:07,953 --> 00:06:10,693
problem” to “I already know the solution.”
